### Native IPv4 Multicast in IPv6 Core
* **Presentation:** [PIM IPv4 in IPv6 core](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-pim-pim-ipv4-in-ipv6-core-00)
* **Presenter:** Stig Venaas
* **Draft Reference:** `draft-ietf-pim-ipv4-in-ipv6-core`
* **Discussion:**
  * Stig Venaas reviewed the recently adopted draft, which enables forwarding native IPv4 multicast traffic over an IPv6 core network without assigning IPv4 addresses to core router interfaces (analogous to RFC 5549 for unicast).
  * IPv4 PIM control messages use IPv6 headers and IPv6 link-local source addresses. To avoid conflict with native IPv6 PIM, a dedicated "All PIM Routers" IPv6 multicast address is proposed.
  * Primary neighbor comparisons, DR elections, Join/Prune targets, and Assert winner selection use IPv6 link-local addresses, while (S,G) group and source addresses remain IPv4.
  * Rendezvous Point (RP) interactions are currently kept out of scope under the assumption that RPs reside outside the core or are reachable via standard unicast mechanism.
  * **Feedback:**
    * Toerless Eckert suggested explicitly scoping the draft to transit core routers, as first-hop and last-hop routers interact directly with RPs and hosts.
    * Ram Kumar asked why operators do not run dual-stack or MVPN. Stig Venaas and Toerless Eckert explained that operators deploying 5549 unicast prefer eliminating IPv4 address management on core interfaces entirely.
    * Mike McBride suggested adding explicit text detailing operator deployment motivations and explaining why alternatives like MVPN may not fit specific native core requirements.

### Deterministic Upstream Neighbor Selection for PIM Joins
* **Presentation:** [Deterministic hashing](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-pim-deterministic-hashing-01)
* **Presenter:** Stig Venaas
* **Draft Reference:** `draft-ietf-pim-deterministic-ecmp`
* **Discussion:**
  * Addresses ECMP hashing inconsistencies in leaf-spine topologies where independent hashing choices at different leaves result in duplicate multicast traffic across multiple spine switches.
  * The draft proposes replacing next-hop IP addresses in the hash with a 32-bit Router ID advertised in PIM Hello messages, ensuring all leaves hash over the same set of IDs.
  * For multi-tier fabrics, a 32-bit "Color" attribute is introduced (PIM Hello Option 43). Leaves hash first on Color and then on Router ID within the selected color.
  * Two hash algorithms were evaluated: Jenkins one-at-a-time (deployed by Arista) and RP Hash from RFC 7761 (implemented by Cisco). Testing indicates RP Hash is faster and exhibits uniform distribution, while Jenkins has operational history.
  * **Feedback:**
    * Hooman Bidgoli noted hashing occurs at signaling time, making execution speed less critical except during reconvergence, and asked about compatibility with Multicast Only Fast Reroute (MOFR). He suggested supporting both algorithms via configuration knobs or Hello negotiation.
    * The working group discussed handling spines that do not advertise a Color; the current proposal ignores non-color spines if at least one spine advertises a Color.
    * Mike McBride and Stig Venaas recommended changing the document status from Informational to Standards Track (Proposed Standard) given protocol option additions and vendor implementations.

### PIM Join Attributes for LISP Environments
* **Presentation:** [draft-ietf-pim-rfc8059-9798bis-02](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-pim-draft-ietf-pim-rfc8059-9798bis-02-00)
* **Presenter:** Prasad Miriyala
* **Draft Reference:** `draft-ietf-pim-rfc8059-9798bis`
* **Discussion:**
  * Consolidates experimental RFC 8059 and RFC 9798 into a single Standards Track document.
  * Enables downstream LISP egress sites to signal preferred transport modes (IPv4/IPv6 address families, unicast or multicast underlays) to upstream ingress sites via PIM Join attributes.
  * Existing IANA-allocated code points are retained.
  * The document serves as a normative reference for `draft-ietf-lisp-rfc6831bis`. A companion deployment experience draft is progressing in the LISP WG.
  * Prasad Miriyala requested Working Group Last Call (WGLC). Stig Venaas requested checking for any open errata on RFC 8059 or RFC 9798 to incorporate prior to WGLC.

### Multicast Lessons Learned
